                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Development has moved to the sequel of this game (and multiplayer appears dead) but while it lasted many weapons and handful of propulsion methods wit various sizes have been added (2 amphibious, 3 ground and omnidirectional rockets in ~2 sizes for flyers). Game contains ballast tanks for submarines but during campaign there is only one aquatic map I think.\n\nSingle player campaign consists mostly of typical rts matches and 2~3 missions where your can't rebuild units. Each mission has custom map. After each mission you unlock new propulsion or weapon. Minimal story in form of text messages during and right before mission. Winning on hard unlocks game plus (all parts avileable form start for you and the enemy).\n\nFor some reason AI is 'afraid' of exploring your base an it task it couple of times to get the courage to destroy you completely.\n\nAlmost no music (single track for combat and single climatic for visiting certain locations), sadly game doesn't incorporate music from trialer).\n\nYou design your own units (havent tried the workshop) but certain actions (likely copy/paste) are not registered by undo/redo (undo delete works). When designing units you must consider center of mass, having enough propulsion for vehicle weight (some weapons are suprisingly heavy), weapon recoil, ground clearance and if theres enough space for wheels to turn. When building flyers you get checklist in the corner for if it can turn (sadly they don't work with diagonal thrusters). \n\nYou can also configure certain propulsion parts behaviour (strength, turn direction and should it powered only for emergency) but its use is necessary only for tiny vehicles. In campaign you gain access to multi/larger-units after ~3 mission.\n\nAs far as part customization goes, you can reshape (bend, twist) structure (basic) parts by selecting action from list in teh editor. Last purple outline structure part on the list has newer system of selecting and dragging corners with mouse (with optional grid). Sadly for as long as part is used by any unit, locks it out from being renamed, removed or reshaped. Because of that, designing list of custom parts at the beginning of the campaign is advised to avoid endless list of custom parts (lucky appearing at the end of the list when filtering by type).\n\nParts and vehicles are stored separately for campaign and main menu with ability to drag units between saves in the main menu.\n\nBase building consists selecting what module to place onto the pad. You get repair/rearm module, labs for unlocking use of certain propulsion and weapons during match, resource converter (special to basic), wall and building tower for placing more pads or new factory.\n\n\nBuy game if you want to support development of its successor (witch fixes part management/creation) or if you really like the idea and are not scared of games part editor.",
